## Conclusion

Across five repeated participant-level experiments, longitudinal information consistently improved discrimination and probability quality for all six prediction targets compared with same-day information alone.

The mean improvements in ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, and Brier score were positive in the expected directions for every target. These improvements were also consistent across the five participant-level experimental repeats, with statistical tests indicating that the observed differences were unlikely to be attributable to random variation within these repeated experiments.

Final held-out test results demonstrated that predictive performance varied substantially across targets. The strongest discrimination was observed for fainting_risk and flare_risk based on ROC-AUC. Need_to_hydrate and need_to_rest produced strong combinations of discrimination and threshold-based classification performance. In contrast, fainting_risk showed substantially weaker positive-class precision, recall, F1, and PR-AUC despite its high ROC-AUC, reflecting the severe class imbalance of that target.

Overall, these findings support the research hypothesis that longitudinal information can provide additional predictive information beyond same-day measurements within the synthetic PaceMate-AI dataset. The results demonstrate the value of incorporating historical information into the experimental prediction framework, while also showing that model performance depends substantially on the prediction target and evaluation metric.

Because the dataset and prediction targets are synthetic, these findings should be interpreted as evidence from a controlled machine-learning experiment rather than evidence of clinical effectiveness. Evaluation using real-world patient data, external validation, prospective testing, and clinical safety assessment would be required before determining whether the approach could provide meaningful clinical utility.